README -- Building GNU Pascal on modern Unix/Linux systems
==========================================================

The GPC sources in this directory ("p/") are a front-end for GCC:
they are grafted into a GCC source tree (`gcc/p/`) and built as part
of GCC.  The last GCC versions GPC supports are the 4.0.x series
(see ../diffs/ for the patches to each supported version).

That makes building GPC on a current Unix/Linux system a two-part
problem: (1) build GCC 4.0.3 with a modern host toolchain, and
(2) keep GPC's own build machinery working with current tools.
Both are solved here:

The build script
----------------

        p/script/modern-build [--prefix=DIR] [--workdir=DIR] [--jobs=N]

downloads the matching GCC "core" sources, grafts this p/ directory
into them, applies GPC's own patch plus the host-compatibility patch
from this directory, builds and installs everything, and runs a
smoke test.  It is safe to re-run; completed steps are skipped.

Example:

        p/script/modern-build --prefix=$HOME/gpc --jobs=8

Afterwards, compile Pascal programs with

        gpc --automake program.pas

(--automake tells GPC to compile used units on demand; this is
GPC's normal way of working.  Used units whose sources are not
installed under the prefix are compiled into the current
directory.)

What was adapted, and why
--------------------------

Host toolchain (GCC >= 10 as the build compiler):

  * CFLAGS used for the whole build: `-O2 -g -fcommon
    -fno-strict-aliasing -std=gnu89'.  `-std=gnu89' is essential:
    GCC's C default changed from gnu89 to gnu17 and later, and with
    it the semantics of `extern inline' (toplev.h would otherwise
    emit duplicate definitions of floor_log2/exact_log2 into every
    object) and the tolerance of implicit declarations in 2006-era
    autoconf tests.  `-fcommon' restores the pre-GCC-10 handling of
    tentative definitions which the old sources rely on.

  * p/Make-lang.in: the front-end object rules now declare the
    generated headers they include (insn-constants.h, tree-check.h,
    insn-modes.h, gtype-desc.h, gcov-iov.h and the gt-p-*.h files)
    as prerequisites, conditionally for GCC 4.x.  Without this,
    `make -j' compiles GPC's sources before the generator programs
    have produced those headers.  The variables must be defined
    before the first rule that uses them because GNU make expands
    prerequisite lists while reading a rule.

  * p/rts/Makefile.in: the run time system is built with `.NOTPARALLEL'
    because GPC's automake facility is not safe to run concurrently
    (parallel gpc1 instances would truncate .gpi files such as
    rtsc.gpi while reading and writing them at the same time).

Host system (current glibc/binutils/distro layout):

  * p/modern/gcc-4.0.3-modern-host.diff: glibc >= 2.28 renamed
    `struct ucontext' to `ucontext_t' and `struct siginfo' to
    `siginfo_t'; libgcc's unwinder (gcc/config/i386/linux-unwind.h)
    uses both and no longer compiles otherwise.

  * The build script installs the system's crt1.o/crti.o/crtn.o and
    the libc.so linker script into $prefix/<target>/lib (and lib/32
    for multilib builds).  GCC 4.0.3 searches /usr/lib64 etc., which
    do not exist on multiarch distributions; the -B search paths it
    does use cover the prefix, which also makes user programs link
    correctly after installation.

  * The one-shot prompts in p/config-lang.in (which patches GCC from
    configure) are fed from /dev/null by the build script.

Notes
-----

  * Documentation (Info files) and man pages are built only if
    makeinfo and help2man are installed; the 2006 texinfo sources
    may not satisfy current makeinfo.  Man page failures during
    `make install' are ignored by GCC's Makefile, as are the doc
    stubs created by the bundled `missing' script.

  * The compiler defaults to 64-bit code generation on x86_64; a
    multilib build also provides 32-bit support if the host has the
    32-bit development files (e.g. libc6-dev-i386 on Debian and
    derivatives).

  * Known-good combination: GCC 4.0.3 with this patch set, built by
    GCC 14.2 on Devuan 6 (glibc 2.41, binutils 2.44), x86_64.
    The same recipe should work with any reasonably recent host.
